  "text": "Pluto showed a world one-fifth the width of Earth can hold water ice mountains, a moon that never sets, and a nitrogen heart with zero craters. Next in our Real World series, we travel inward to Neptune's largest moon, Triton, a captured Kuiper Belt cousin of Pluto that orbits backwards and erupts active geysers on a frozen nitrogen crust at minus two hundred and thirty-five degrees Celsius. Looking back from two hundred thousand kilometers away on 14th of July, 2015, New Horizons captured its farewell color portrait of Pluto's receding crescent and blue atmospheric haze.",
